                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9adf3675-9ec1-3b15-bca9-9304e991c902",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Prohibited C Function Identified\" is a vulnerability that occurs when a program uses a function that is considered unsafe due to its potential for causing buffer overflow. In C++, this often includes functions like `strcpy()`, `sprintf()`, `vsprintf()`, `gets()`, etc. These functions do not perform bounds checking, and can therefore overwrite memory, leading to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or code execution.\n\nIn the provided code, `vsnprintf()` is used, which is generally considered safer than `sprintf()` or `vsprintf()`, because it takes a size argument to prevent buffer overflow. However, it can still be misused or cause issues if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives whenever possible. In C++, this often means using the string class and its methods, which automatically handle memory management and prevent buffer overflow.\n\nIf you must use C-style strings and functions, always ensure that you are correctly managing memory and checking bounds. Never assume that input will be a certain size or format.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIn the provided code, `vsnprintf()` is used correctly, as it includes a size argument. However, to further improve safety, you could use a `std::string` and `vsnprintf()` together, like so:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <memory>\n#include <cstdarg>\n\nvoid safe_printf(const char* msg, ...) {\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, msg);\n    size_t size = vsnprintf(nullptr, 0, msg, args) + 1; // Find out the length\n    va_end(args);\n\n    std::unique_ptr<char[]> buff_ptr(new char[size]);\n\n    va_start(args, msg);\n    vsnprintf(buff_ptr.get(), size, msg, args); // Write to buffer\n    va_end(args);\n\n    std::string safe_string = std::string(buff_ptr.get(), size - 1); // Convert to std::string\n}\n```\n\nThis code first finds out the length of the formatted string, then allocates a buffer of the correct size, writes to it, and finally converts it to a `std::string`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<string>`\n- `<memory>`\n- `<cstdarg>`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6dadbb4a-57b7-3127-b32f-5d314281873d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memcpy operation. The destination is an array of 32 doubles, and the source is calculated by offsetting a pointer, which may overlap with the destination. Dataflow analysis suggests that the destination and source may reference overlapping memory, which is not allowed for memcpy and can result in unpredictable behavior. Additionally, there is a risk that either the source or destination pointer could be null, as there are no explicit checks to prevent this. These factors indicate a real risk of memory corruption or program crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when using memcpy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. Add explicit checks before the memcpy call to verify that the memory regions are distinct. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif ((uintptr_t)_floats + sizeof(_floats) <= (uintptr_t)registers ||\n    (uintptr_t)registers + sizeof(_registers) <= (uintptr_t)_floats) {\n    memcpy(_floats, static_cast<const uint8_t *>(registers) + sizeof(_registers), sizeof(_floats));\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use memmove or report an error\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy operation.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf there is a possibility of overlap between the source and destination, use memmove instead of memcpy, as memmove is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(_floats, static_cast<const uint8_t *>(registers) + sizeof(_registers), sizeof(_floats));\n```\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the memory regions overlap.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ba5ac619-5a7c-3b6f-a4c6-2ef06ca3a6c8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The verdict is supported by the classification indicating undefined behavior risk and the explicit 'GENUINE' verdict. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions, which is not allowed for the standard memory copy function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a possibility that either the source or destination pointer could be null, as there are no clear checks or guards in place to prevent this. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. You can add a runtime check to verify that the memory ranges are either completely disjoint or use a safer function if overlap is possible.\n\n```cpp\nif ((Arr.data() + ArrSize <= data()) || (data() + ArrSize <= Arr.data())) {\n    std::memcpy(Arr.data(), data(), ArrSize);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case, e.g., use memmove or log an error\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf there is any possibility that the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy function with a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as `memmove`:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(Arr.data(), data(), ArrSize);\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
