            {
              "id": "d9060b02-2029-3d18-ac8a-792e39d0201f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of a malloc call is used without a preceding null check. The allocation uses sizeof(_Unwind_Exception) as the size, which is a typical pattern, but there is no guard to ensure that the allocation succeeded before the pointer is dereferenced or used as a struct pointer (first use: ptr-field). The absence of a null guard before the first use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate this risk. The call verdict is 'GENUINE', confirming this is a real and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a possible null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated pointer. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nexc = (_Unwind_Exception *)malloc(sizeof(_Unwind_Exception));\nexc->field = ...; // unsafe if malloc fails\n```\n\nYou should add a null check immediately after the allocation:\n\n```c\nexc = (_Unwind_Exception *)malloc(sizeof(_Unwind_Exception));\nif (exc == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return an error or abort\n    return ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;\n}\nexc->field = ...; // safe to use exc now\n```\n\nThis ensures that the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash. For more information on secure memory handling in C, see the following references.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
