              "id": "75073958-f796-3f00-9459-8afc5924363a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if `oldSize * sizeof(entry)` is larger than the size of `newBuffer`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution. An attacker could exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the data and the size of the buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy` such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t bufferSize = oldSize * sizeof(entry);\nif (bufferSize <= sizeof(newBuffer)) {\n    memcpy(newBuffer, _buffer, bufferSize);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first calculate the size of the data being copied and then check if it is larger than the size of the destination buffer. If it is, we handle the error instead of calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1117cd6f-597e-3d3c-965c-bcdebca9a901",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a multiplication, as in the provided code snippet, and one of the operands is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to an integer overflow. This overflow can cause the `malloc` function to allocate less memory than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and limit the size of the input that is used in the `malloc` function. You should also consider using functions that can handle integer overflows safely, such as `calloc`. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `malloc` function with the `calloc` function, which is designed to handle integer overflows. The `calloc` function takes two arguments: the number of elements and the size of each element. It also initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n\n```cpp\n// Old vulnerable code\n// entry *data = (entry*) malloc(newSize * sizeof(entry));\n\n// New fixed code\nentry *data = (entry*) calloc(newSize, sizeof(entry));\nif (data == NULL) {\n    // handle memory allocation failure\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` and `calloc` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "09f61ed9-1f72-3047-acc1-e5bd2e089798",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, it can lead to buffer overflow attacks, memory corruption, and other security issues. \n\nIn the given code snippet, `malloc(sizeof(PrologInfoStackEntry))`, the size of `PrologInfoStackEntry` is used to allocate memory. If the size of `PrologInfoStackEntry` is not properly controlled or validated, it can lead to the aforementioned vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and control the size of the memory to be allocated. Avoid using user input directly as the size for memory allocation. Also, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nPrologInfoStackEntry* entry = NULL;\nentry = (PrologInfoStackEntry*) malloc(sizeof(PrologInfoStackEntry));\nif (entry == NULL) {\n    // Handle malloc failure\n    // ...\n} else {\n    // Continue with normal processing\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first initialize the pointer to `NULL`. Then we allocate memory using `malloc` and check if the memory allocation was successful by comparing the returned pointer with `NULL`. If `malloc` fails, it returns `NULL` and we handle the failure accordingly.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the given code snippet.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5d562cc4-32a1-35a5-b15b-7334418a6154",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen(ptr)` function in C++ is used to calculate the length of a string. The vulnerability arises when the pointer `ptr` points to a memory location that does not contain a null-terminated string. In such cases, `strlen(ptr)` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null byte, potentially leading to buffer overflows, segmentation faults, or other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the pointer passed to `strlen()` points to a null-terminated string. If the string's length is known at compile time, consider using that value directly instead of calling `strlen()`. If the string's length is not known at compile time, consider using safer alternatives such as `strnlen()`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past that length.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `ptr` is a pointer to a character array that is not null-terminated, you can fix the vulnerability by ensuring that the array is null-terminated before passing it to `strlen()`. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// ...\n\nchar arr[100];\nstd::fill(arr, arr + sizeof(arr), '\\0');  // Fill the array with null bytes\n// Write some data to arr...\nstd::strncpy(arr, \"Hello, world!\", sizeof(arr) - 1);  // Ensure null termination\nsize_t len = std::strlen(arr);  // Now this is safe\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c7ccfd08-05f6-366e-8f9e-888820cc94b9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e6e29722-3cdf-3508-ae60-8df0fdfda27b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `vsscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used carefully.\n\nThe `vsscanf` function reads data from `__s` and stores them according to parameter format into the locations pointed by the elements in the variable argument list identified by `__va`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. For instance, instead of `vsscanf`, consider using `sscanf_s` or `fscanf_s` which are more secure versions of the function. These functions require an additional parameter which is the size of the buffer, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vsscanf` function with `sscanf_s` or `fscanf_s`. Here is an example of how to use `sscanf_s`:\n\n```cpp\nchar __s[] = \"123 456\";\nint a, b;\nint num = sscanf_s(__s, \"%d %d\", &a, &b);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `vsscanf` function is part of the C Standard Library, so you need to include the `stdio.h` or `cstdio` header file.\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5374c33-0c7b-3d7b-8213-8a894365b9d6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `vsnprintf()` is used, which is a safer alternative to `sprintf()`, but can still lead to vulnerabilities if not used correctly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to use these functions in a way that ensures that buffer overflows cannot occur. This can be done by always ensuring that the size argument passed to these functions is less than or equal to the size of the buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer way to use `vsnprintf()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdarg.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nvoid safe_printf(const char *format, ...) {\n    char buffer[1024];\n    va_list args;\n    va_start(args, format);\n    vsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), format, args);\n    va_end(args);\n    printf(\"%s\", buffer);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `vsnprintf()` is used in a way that ensures that the size of the buffer is never exceeded, preventing buffer overflows.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `stdarg.h`: for handling variable argument list functions.\n- `stdio.h`: for input/output operations.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1dbf5fbc-d6b5-3251-b8c9-996c405cd073",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `mbsrtowcs()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\nThe `mbsrtowcs()` function converts a sequence of multibyte characters to a sequence of wide characters. If the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the converted characters, a buffer overflow can occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Use safer alternatives whenever possible. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the converted characters plus a null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `mbsrtowcs()` with a safer alternative such as `mbsrtowcs_s()`, which includes a parameter for the size of the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\n#include <locale.h>\n#include <wchar.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nsize_t len = mbstowcs(NULL, __src, 0) + 1;\nwchar_t* __dest = (wchar_t*)malloc(len*sizeof(wchar_t));\nmbstate_t __ps;\nconst char* temp = __src;\nmbsrtowcs(__dest, &temp, len, &__ps);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- locale.h\n- wchar.h\n- stdlib.h\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bf0c001f-6030-3393-b143-3f04aa7b2499",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `wcrtomb(__s, __wc, __ps)` is used, which converts a wide character to a multibyte character. This function can lead to buffer overflow if not used properly.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that are known to be insecure. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. Always validate and sanitize input data. Be aware of the size of the data types and the size of the buffer where data is being stored.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe `wcrtomb` function can be replaced with `std::wcrtomb` function which is safer. Here is an example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <locale>\n#include <codecvt>\n#include <string>\n\nstd::wstring_convert<std::codecvt_utf8<wchar_t>> converter;\nstd::string multibyte_string = converter.to_bytes(wide_character);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- locale\n- codecvt\n- string\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "89019f3c-cc47-3ba5-ac5b-c30cf3cde4e9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming language refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other problems. One such function is `printf`.\n\nThe `printf` function is a common function in C and C++ for outputting text. However, it can be dangerous if used improperly, especially when handling user-supplied input. This is because `printf` can interpret certain special characters (like `%s`, `%d`, etc.) and if an attacker can control the format string, they can potentially exploit this to execute arbitrary code, read from or write to arbitrary memory locations, or cause the program to crash.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `printf` with user-supplied input. Instead, use safer alternatives that do not interpret format strings, such as `puts` or `fputs`. If you need to format strings, use functions like `snprintf` that limit the amount of output, and always use a static format string.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nprintf(user_input);\n```\n\nUse:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nputs(user_input);\n```\n\nOr, if you need to format strings:\n\n```cpp\nchar* user_input = get_user_input();\nchar buffer[256];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), \"%s\", user_input);\nputs(buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code examples above require the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
